:11.932,"text":"The first is git reflog. It's a diary of everywhere your branch has been, so after a bad reset, you find the old commit and jump straight back."},{"id":"i1b","start":12.032,"end":14.863,"text":"By default, Git keeps those lost commits for thirty days."},{"id":"i2","start":14.963,"end":20.262,"text":"The second is git restore. Broke a file you haven't committed? One command puts it back the way it was before your edits."},{"id":"i3","start":20.362,"end":26.615,"text":"The third is git stash. Halfway through something and need to switch branches? Stash parks your changes, and stash pop brings them back."},{"id":"i4","start":26.715,"end":33.964,"text":"And finally, git revert. Already pushed a bad commit to GitHub? Don't rewrite history. Revert adds a new commit that undoes it, so nobody else's work breaks."},{"id":"s1","start":34.064,"end":37.523,"text":"Reflog, restore, stash and revert. That's your undo button."},{"id":"c1","start":37.623,"end":42.411,"text":"If you want to practise real code like this, a few minutes a day, the link's in my bio. It's called Teyro."}],"lip":{"amp":[0,0,0,0,0.001,0.038,1,1,1,1,1,0.702,1,1,0.484,0.928,1,1,1,1,0.705,0.529,1,0.948,0.474,0.011,0.548,0.796,1,0.863,0.957,0.919,1,0.669,1,0.422,0.032,0.002,0.001,0.026,0.718,1,1,1,0.188,0.394,0.842,0.82,0.781,1,0.934,0.298,0.454,1,0.365,0.418,1,0.612,0.338,0.57,1,0.644,0.08,0.083,1,1,1,0.067,0.203,0.192,0.007,0.001,0,0,0.001,0.001,0.035,0.901,1,1,1,1,1,0.72,0.279,0.135,0.601,1,1,0.921,0.251,0.283,0.803,1,1,0.91,0.741,0.771,0.774,0.706,1,1,0.695,0.138,0.881,0.931,0.578,0.3,0.707,0.852,0.88,1,1,0.832,1,1,0.937,1,1,0.827,0.891,0.999,0.984,0.897,0.7,0.513,0.388,0.545,0.912,0.555,0.56,0.667,0.605,0.058,0.744,0.856,0.749,0.623,0.859,1,0.885,0.538,0.077,0.002,0,0,0.001,0.003,0.005,0.673,0.776,0.396,0.324,0.988,0.967,0.904,0.94,0.742,0.861,0.683,0.387,0.543,0.593,1,0.859,0.766,0.845,0.364,0.008,0.024,0.67,1,1,1,0.301,0.083,0.191,0.715,1,1,0.989,0.553,0.293,0.423,0.615,1,1,1,1,0.907,0.9